>There is water, holy water, and unholy water. So does that mean
>cursed/uncursed/blessed status does not apply to water? As in, you
>can't have cursed holy water, uncursed holy water, or blessed holy
>water - you just have holy water (though going through the blessing
>process for normal water turns it into holy water). 

  Correct.
            
  Blessed water:
   - is the same as holy water
   - will not wet or dilute anything dipped into it.
   - can only bless something dipped into it.
     - dipped cursed item goes to uncursed
       - "glows amber(?)"
     - dipped uncursed item goes to blessed
       - "glows blue(?)"
     - dipped blessed item has no effect
       - in this case only the holy water is not used up.

  Cursed water:
   - is the same as unholy water
   - will not wet or dilute anything dipped into it.
   - can only cursed something dipped into it.
     - dipped blessed item goes to uncursed
       - "glows brown(?)"
     - dipped uncursed item goes to cursed
       - "glows black(?)"
     - dipped cursed item has no effect
       - in this case only the unholy water is not used up.

  Uncursed water:
   - will dilute dilutable items dipped into it.
   - will blank blankable items dipped into it.
   - will rust rustable items dipped into it.
   - In all cases the uncursed water will be used up.

>What if one dips an
>unidentified, cursed potion into a potion of water, twice. What will
>the cursed potion turn into?

  Some guessing is involved in the following:

  It will depend on whether the other potion is:
    - cursed (i.e. unholy water)
      - no effect 
      - You'll get the message: "Interesting"
    - uncursed
      - Normally you'd get a diluted potion.  I'd bet on the no 
        effect, "Interesting" result as above.
    - blessed
      - You'll end up with
        - the original cursed potion (i.e. unholy water) being blessed, 
        - using up the blessed water (i.e. holy water).
        - resulting in an uncursed potion of water.

